From b2502960da058bb8dbffcb9f1e3576a8d576ae0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <nbw.designs@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:08:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] syscall, os, internal/poll, time: enough of the standard
 library for fmt

A first cut of the packages between fmt and the runtime, sized by
building a fmt program and following the errors, and modelled file by
file on Plan 9 -- the other port with no Unix syscalls.

syscall: Read/Write/Open/Close/Seek through functions the runtime
exports (runtime/syscall_aros.go), each an asmcgocall into the C
library wrapped in entersyscall/exitsyscall as on Darwin. Errno is the
C library's number, with the common Unix values named. Everything a
fmt program does not touch is ENOSYS and says so.

os: File keeps its own descriptor and does I/O straight through syscall
(Plan 9's shape; internal/poll is used for its refcounting mutex only).
Stat, readdir, process control, links, chmod: ENOSYS. DevNull is NIL:,
tempDir is T:.

internal/poll: an FD with no poller behind it. time: UTC only for now.
internal/filepathlite: AROS paths -- VOLUME: makes a path absolute, a
leading '/' means the parent directory and is relative.

Build tags: env_unix.go, removeall_noat.go, statat_other.go and
root_noopenat.go gain aros; rawconn.go and types_unix.go exclude it, as
they exclude plan9.

