+From 0dc40452c44cb82d8fbacacd33590765f265370a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: tomaszstaniak <nbw.designs@gmail.com>
+Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 13:28:02 +0200
+Subject: [PATCH 4/7] cmd/link, runtime/cgo: link a Go program into an AROS
+ executable
+
+External linking works. Go's linker writes go.o and x86_64-aros-gcc
+produces an ET_REL that AROS's own LoadSeg accepts and relocates --
+verified on a running AROS One with segdump, whose first-movabs check
+resolved to "dos.library". Twelve hunks, 231 mov %gs:0x18,%r14 loads in
+the final text, and no ELF TLS relocations.
+
+Four things stood between the relocation size mismatch and that:
+
+- extreloc had to agree with relocsym that R_TLS_LE is internal on
+  AROS. The counting pass excluded it and the emitting pass did not, so
+  elfrelocsect wrote 239 relocations more than it had room for.
+- -rdynamic is rejected by the AROS gcc driver, and is meaningless for
+  an ET_REL nobody dlopens; skipped in hostlink.
+- runtime/cgo needed an AROS thread file. pthread_unix.c cannot be
+  reused because AROS has no sigfillset or pthread_sigmask -- signals
+  there are Exec's per-task bits, not Unix masks. gcc_aros_amd64.c is
+  that file with the mask dance removed; pthread_getattr_np, which
+  AROS does provide, keeps the stack-bound path.
+- rt0 is two jumps and settls is a bare RET, as on Plan 9: the kernel
+  publishes the thread pointer, so the runtime has nothing to install.
+
+Known leftover: the linker still emits runtime.tlsg into a .tbss
+section. Nothing references it and LoadSeg did not object, but it
+should not be there.
+
